WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:13.740
Dear friends of VisualPolitik, I think most
of you will agree: achieving a strong democracy

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:15.150
isn’t easy.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:20.880
In our world, there are many democracies,
but unfortunately, few quality ones.

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:25.290
Obviously when I speak of quality I mean a
democratic system that respects individual

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:32.500
freedom, legal security, political plurality...
things like that.

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:43.560
Well, when it comes down to it, there's one
basic principle that's essential for any quality

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:44.560
democracy.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:47.970
And isn’t usually liked by most politicians
at all.

00:00:47.970 --> 00:00:52.450
I’m talking about alternation; something
that’s essential in order to stop any single

00:00:52.450 --> 00:01:03.970
person or group of people from essentially
taking over government institutions.

00:01:03.970 --> 00:01:16.360
It’s precisely due to this concern that
many countries have a limitation of mandates.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:22.200
“Nothing is as dangerous as allowing the
same citizen to stay in power for a long time.

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:27.640
The people get used to obeying him and him
to leading them, from here, usurpation and

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.080
tyranny emerge.”

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:30.300
Simón Bolívar)

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:35.930
Folks, trying to prevent political alternation
at any cost isn’t new, or rare, throughout

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:36.930
the world.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:41.350
And in recent years, in one way or another,
Latin America has been plagued with politicians

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:44.679
who’ve managed to retain power at all costs.

00:01:44.679 --> 00:01:50.510
Politicians like Daniel Ortega in Nicaragua,
Juan Orlando Hernández in Honduras and Nicolás

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.939
Maduro in Venezuela.

00:01:52.939 --> 00:01:58.490
Now, the latest person to join this list seems
to be the Bolivian President, Evo Morales.

00:01:58.490 --> 00:02:05.440
And the question many of you are currently
asking is, is Bolivian democracy in danger?

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:07.200
What’s happening?

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.959
Why is Evo Morales’s candidacy so controversial?

00:02:11.959 --> 00:02:16.440
Listen up.

00:02:16.440 --> 00:02:23.760
(PROMISES AND AN ADDICTION TO POWER)

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:29.840
On January 22nd 2006, with more than 50% of
the vote, Evo Morales became the first indigenous

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:35.320
president in the 193 years since Bolivia’s
independence.

00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:40.060
His arrival to power meant the end of political
exclusion for most of Bolivian society.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:47.209
In fact, from a historical perspective, I
think Evo’s rise to power was inevitable.

00:02:47.209 --> 00:02:54.151
But with his leftist speech against Yankee
imperialism and in favor of the political

00:02:54.151 --> 00:02:58.670
participation of indigenous peoples and the
nationalization of natural resources, Evo

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:04.010
Morales set out to lead a transformation in
the country, a transformation that had wide

00:03:04.010 --> 00:03:05.349
popular support.

00:03:05.349 --> 00:03:09.970
To achieve his objectives, the Bolivian government
opted for something that has become very fashionable

00:03:09.970 --> 00:03:13.960
in Latin America in recent years: Changing
the Constitution!

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:18.860
Something that, by the way, also gained a
lot of popular support, as election after

00:03:18.860 --> 00:03:24.850
election, Evo Morales swept the polls.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:31.099
But... let's go back to the Constitution,
to Evo Morales’ own Constitution.

00:03:31.099 --> 00:03:35.400
This is what his article 168 said.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:41.790
“The State’s President or Vice President
mandate lasts for 5 years and they can be

00:03:41.790 --> 00:03:44.450
re-elected only once on a continuous basis.”

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:47.159
Article 168 of the Bolivian Constitution)

00:03:47.159 --> 00:03:49.290
It's clear, right?

00:03:49.290 --> 00:03:54.050
On a continuous basis, a person can only exercise
the office of president for two terms.

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:56.770
And each term lasts 5 years.

00:03:56.770 --> 00:04:02.180
Thus, as Evo ran for re-election in 2009,
his time in government should’ve ended in

00:04:02.180 --> 00:04:05.400
2014, right?

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:11.909
Basic mathematics.

00:04:11.909 --> 00:04:32.569
But, but... wait... something doesn’t fit.

00:04:32.569 --> 00:04:39.449
It’s the year 2018, Evo Morales is still
in power and is going to run for re-election

00:04:39.449 --> 00:04:42.139
in the 2019 elections.

00:04:42.139 --> 00:04:45.639
So, what the hell happened?

00:04:45.639 --> 00:04:50.280
Listen up.

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:54.270
(THE INDEFINITE REELECTION)

00:04:54.270 --> 00:05:00.270
Folks, this is the story of how a man fell
in love with Power.

00:05:00.270 --> 00:05:06.740
Let's see, Evo’s Constitution established
alternation as a necessary principle for democracy

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:07.740
to work.

00:05:07.740 --> 00:05:10.900
But... as soon as the new Constitution was
approved, problems began.

00:05:10.900 --> 00:05:15.860
Evo said ok, the previous mandate didn’t
count because it happened under a different

00:05:15.860 --> 00:05:20.149
Constitution... so just as the Bolivian state
had been re-founded they needed to reset the

00:05:20.149 --> 00:05:21.659
score to zero.

00:05:21.659 --> 00:05:24.960
Yeah, it’s a bit absurd.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:29.619
Alternation isn’t a punishment, but a way
of preventing a person from being in power

00:05:29.619 --> 00:05:30.710
for too long.

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:35.430
And if you believe in it and introduce it
into the Constitution that you yourself have

00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:41.190
promoted... well you’ll need to respect
it, right?

00:05:41.190 --> 00:05:42.190
Well…

00:05:42.190 --> 00:05:43.190
No.

00:05:43.190 --> 00:05:49.029
In fact, Evo Morales managed to get a positive
ruling from the Constitutional Court on a

00:05:49.029 --> 00:05:50.599
very controversial case.

00:05:50.599 --> 00:05:55.430
According to the court, Evo had been the President
of Bolivia, but now he wanted to be the president

00:05:55.430 --> 00:06:01.379
of the Plurinational State of Bolivia, which
isn’t the same, so he could govern for two

00:06:01.379 --> 00:06:02.770
consecutive terms.

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:05.589
See the difference?

00:06:05.589 --> 00:06:11.229
Overnight Bolivia, the Bolivia that had always
existed, had disappeared.

00:06:11.229 --> 00:06:15.990
This explains why Evo ran in the 2009 elections
and for re-election in 2014.

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:19.499
But no, of course, he wouldn’t be able to
run again.

00:06:19.499 --> 00:06:22.379
And actually, Evo himself has said this plenty
of times.

00:06:22.379 --> 00:06:27.870
He said that in 2019 he’d leave the world
of politics to open his own restaurant.

00:06:27.870 --> 00:06:40.180
But... just a second… because yes, the restaurant
sounds great, but staying in power?

00:06:40.180 --> 00:06:45.319
Folks, that sounded a lot better to Evo.

00:06:45.319 --> 00:06:49.589
So in 2015, just one year after his re-election,
deputies related to the President, presented

00:06:49.589 --> 00:06:51.990
a proposal to reform the Constitution.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:54.300
The newly approved Constitution!

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:57.219
Can you guess what for?

00:06:57.219 --> 00:07:01.879
So that both the president and vice-president
could be reelected two times, that is, so

00:07:01.879 --> 00:07:05.110
that Evo can run again in 2019.

00:07:05.110 --> 00:07:07.509
However, this time the shot backfired.

00:07:07.509 --> 00:07:12.050
The reform did go ahead in Parliament... but
it also had to be approved by referendum...

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:20.699
and there, surprisingly, Evo lost.

00:07:20.699 --> 00:07:27.709
Do you agree with the reform of article 168
of the Political Constitution of the State

00:07:27.709 --> 00:07:32.469
so that the President and Vice President of
the State can be reelected twice in a continuous

00:07:32.469 --> 00:07:33.469
way?)

00:07:33.469 --> 00:07:36.689
The question of the 2016 constitutional referendum)

00:07:36.689 --> 00:07:41.490
The question was clear and the answers even
more so: Yes or No.

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:44.069
Yes or no to reelection.

00:07:44.069 --> 00:07:45.749
And the Bolivians said No,

00:07:45.749 --> 00:07:48.149
But what do you think Evo Morales did?

00:07:48.149 --> 00:07:52.719
Did he accept the result of the referendum
and the Constitution that he himself had embraced

00:07:52.719 --> 00:07:54.130
and promoted?

00:07:54.130 --> 00:07:55.439
Well... obviously not.

00:07:55.439 --> 00:07:59.539
The indigenous leader, the politician who
was going to return the sovereignty to the

00:07:59.539 --> 00:08:05.239
people, suddenly came to say: alright, let’s
see… the people have been manipulated...

00:08:05.239 --> 00:08:07.659
so they don’t really need to be taken into
account.

00:08:07.659 --> 00:08:11.599
However, in spite of everything, he once again
promised to respect the results.

00:08:11.599 --> 00:08:15.619
Now, this time, was he serious or not?

00:08:15.619 --> 00:08:16.619
Well…

00:08:16.619 --> 00:08:18.469
No, he wasn’t.

00:08:18.469 --> 00:08:22.659
See, taking advantage of his control over
the institutions – which is precisely what

00:08:22.659 --> 00:08:26.219
alternation tries to avoid – Evo managed
to do something unheard of:

00:08:26.219 --> 00:08:31.009
The “Plurinational Constitutional Court”,
the body tasked with interpreting fundamental

00:08:31.009 --> 00:08:36.750
rule, in its 84/2017 ruling, declared parts
of the Constitution itself as invalid.

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:41.279
To justify such a measure, they used International
Law, and the Constitutional Court devoted

00:08:41.279 --> 00:08:46.040
itself to assessing and interpreting the American
Convention on Human Rights, the Inter-American

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:52.430
Court Jurisprudence... everything but the
National Constitution itself.

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:56.750
In the end, the result of this entire experiment
was that the articles that bothered Evo Morales,

00:08:56.750 --> 00:09:02.500
from his own Constitution, were de facto repealed
and it was ruled, listen to this, that indefinite

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:05.730
re-elections were a human right.

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:11.880
Because limiting mandates and demanding alternation
is an attempt to undermine human rights.

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:13.779
No, in the end, Evo will even get Álvaro
Uribe to support his regime.

00:09:13.779 --> 00:09:16.720
In short, let’s get serious... because it’s
no joke that a politician can do and undo

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:20.380
whatever he pleases with his country’s institutions.

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:24.490
That is dangerous, very dangerous.

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:29.470
The fact is that in Bolivia, Article 168 hasn’t
been repealed or modified.

00:09:29.470 --> 00:09:35.870
What happened is that now the Bolivian Constitution
has parts that are upheld and others that

00:09:35.870 --> 00:09:36.930
aren’t.

00:09:36.930 --> 00:09:41.699
All of this has unleashed protests against
Evo Morales’s anti-democratic drift, both

00:09:41.699 --> 00:09:43.129
inside and outside of the country.

00:09:43.129 --> 00:09:47.279
Of course, in the face of such a spectacle,
several members of the Electoral Tribunal

00:09:47.279 --> 00:09:51.440
have resigned, and have been replaced by people
close to Evo Morales’s own party.

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:56.699
Thus, finally on December 4th, 2018, this
body approved Evo Morales’s candidacy to

00:09:56.699 --> 00:10:00.770
the presidency and Álvaro Garcia Linera to
the Vice Presidency.

00:10:00.770 --> 00:10:06.630
“Nothing is as dangerous as allowing the
same citizen to stay in power for a long time.

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:12.079
The people get used to obeying him and him
to leading them, from here, usurpation and

00:10:12.079 --> 00:10:13.509
tyranny emerge.”

00:10:13.509 --> 00:10:14.790
Simón Bolívar)

00:10:14.790 --> 00:10:16.029
But... just a second.

00:10:16.029 --> 00:10:18.069
I know what some of you may be thinking...

00:10:18.069 --> 00:10:23.910
But Simon, there will still be elections...
if the Bolivians don’t want him then they

00:10:23.910 --> 00:10:26.079
shouldn’t vote for him, right?

00:10:26.079 --> 00:10:28.880
Well, folks, there are two problems with this:

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:34.290
First, a democracy can never, ever consist
of tyranny of the majority over the minority.

00:10:34.290 --> 00:10:38.680
Institutions need to be respected and their
mechanisms must exist to prevent a politician

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:43.439
from clinging to power through the use of
techniques such as clientelistic voting, dependence

00:10:43.439 --> 00:10:47.029
on the government, control of the media or
simple coercion.

00:10:47.029 --> 00:10:53.019
The second problem has a lot to do with the
obsession with power that Evo Morales has

00:10:53.019 --> 00:10:54.589
shown.

00:10:54.589 --> 00:11:03.140
In other words: how far are Evo Morales and
his party, the Movement to Socialism, willing

00:11:03.140 --> 00:11:10.480
to go to stay in power?

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:12.839
Evo lost the Referendum and is still running.

00:11:12.839 --> 00:11:16.180
He’s moved heaven and earth to be a candidate
again.

00:11:16.180 --> 00:11:21.079
So now the question is, will he be willing
to lose the elections or will he opt instead

00:11:21.079 --> 00:11:23.470
for the strategy of winning at any cost?

00:11:23.470 --> 00:11:27.189
I’m referring to the strategy that has kept
people like Nicolás Maduro, Daniel Ortega

00:11:27.189 --> 00:11:29.339
and Juan Orlando Hernández in power.

00:11:29.339 --> 00:11:31.370
Come on, the usual move.

00:11:31.370 --> 00:11:34.380
This is the question that many are asking
themselves in Bolivia.

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:39.030
And watch out, because what Evo Morales has
achieved, besides being the Bolivian politician

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:43.250
who’s been in power the longest, is getting
this country’s institutions to act at his

00:11:43.250 --> 00:11:44.750
will.

00:11:44.750 --> 00:11:45.769
Bolivians declare Evo Morales as “the new
dictator”.

00:11:45.769 --> 00:11:48.720
More than 150 civil organizations agreed to
declare Evo Morales as a dictator and determined

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:50.389
an activities agenda in “defense of democracy”.

00:11:50.389 --> 00:11:51.389
PanamPost)

